they used their IDE or something

00:08:54.450 --> 00:08:56.625
like that. And so,

00:08:57.570 --> 00:09:00.390
I see the benefits
of both video

00:09:01.050 --> 00:09:03.495
for communication and
teaching and all that, and

00:09:04.560 --> 00:09:05.060
written.

00:09:05.580 --> 00:09:07.410
And written is certainly
my first choice.

00:09:07.410 --> 00:09:09.750
And so you'll notice
all my screencasts,

00:09:10.440 --> 00:09:12.390
I pop up a quick blog post.

00:09:12.390 --> 00:09:14.520
I don't put a ton of energy
into it, but I always--

00:09:15.180 --> 00:09:17.710
there's always a place
you can go to see it.

00:09:18.360 --> 00:09:20.565
I also do that partly
because I have had people

00:09:21.210 --> 00:09:22.650
talk to me about the

00:09:24.000 --> 00:09:28.110
accessibility issues around
video for communication. And if

00:09:28.110 --> 00:09:31.425
you have something that is text that's HTML

00:09:32.130 --> 00:09:35.646
on a website that's
much more accessible to

00:09:37.350 --> 00:09:41.140
people who need to use
screen readers or have--

00:09:42.060 --> 00:09:44.410
so I always try to do both.
I always try to do both.

00:09:45.390 --> 00:09:48.270
So at the beginning, I was
like, let's do an experiment. Let's see.

00:09:48.270 --> 00:09:52.200
And I think it really helped
people see tidymodels as

00:09:52.200 --> 00:09:55.380
something that was like, oh, I
can try that. Let me do that.

00:09:55.380 --> 00:09:56.080
Let me do that.

00:09:56.080 --> 00:10:00.180
And so that would be what went
into the start of it. And then

00:10:00.180 --> 00:10:01.980
certainly, as I've moved--

00:10:01.980 --> 00:10:05.820
depending on the balance of my time
in every given month, I don't always--

00:10:05.820 --> 00:10:08.070
my goal is to do two a month,

00:10:08.070 --> 00:10:09.810
but I don't always
hit two a month.

00:10:10.792 --> 00:10:11.150
-

00:10:11.150 --> 00:10:15.290
I really like having both
the video and blog format too

00:10:15.290 --> 00:10:17.975
because if you get
stuck in either one,

00:10:18.680 --> 00:10:21.470
you go to the other
and unblocks you.

00:10:21.470 --> 00:10:21.970
- Yeah.

00:10:22.910 --> 00:10:26.220
- I see Ben. You had a
question in the Zoom chat.

00:10:26.220 --> 00:10:27.125
Do you want to jump in here?

00:10:27.125 --> 00:10:27.725
- Yeah.

00:10:28.690 --> 00:10:30.740
Hey, Julia. I'm curious,

00:10:30.740 --> 00:10:34.550
you've obviously been on
long term to value projects--

00:10:34.550 --> 00:10:35.370
longer term--

00:10:35.370 --> 00:10:36.710
so you're not able to see--

00:10:36.710 --> 00:10:38.330
the benchmark is
just really high.

00:10:38.330 --> 00:10:40.715
And I have a couple that
I'm working on as well

00:10:41.330 --> 00:10:43.310
for an internal solution.

00:10:43.310 --> 00:10:46.700
And it's hard to maintain
the excitement, also, to create

00:10:48.260 --> 00:10:52.350
that vision for the end
goal and also to vet it out.

00:10:52.350 --> 00:10:54.890
So I have five
different questions built into this.

00:10:54.890 --> 00:10:56.720
- OK. Let's see if I
can remember them all.

00:10:57.720 --> 00:10:58.655
Let's do them one at a time.

00:10:59.260 --> 00:11:01.160
- So the idea is--

00:11:01.160 --> 00:11:03.310
the main one is,
how do you maintain

00:11:04.430 --> 00:11:08.960
excitement and enthusiasm but
also maintain a clear scope

00:11:08.960 --> 00:11:11.620
over a long term
to value project?

00:11:11.620 --> 00:11:12.300
- Yeah.

00:11:13.860 --> 00:11:15.075
So this

00:11:17.520 --> 00:11:18.920
is a great question.

00:11:19.920 --> 00:11:22.410
I think that the number 1

00:11:23.130 --> 00:11:24.402
thing is

00:11:26.010 --> 00:11:26.510
to--

00:11:28.725 --> 00:11:31.230
I know you all have seen
these kind of pictures.

00:11:31.230 --> 00:11:32.767
You can think about waterfall

00:11:34.140 --> 00:11:35.085
versus MVP.

00:11:35.670 --> 00:11:38.580
And you've seen those pictures
of making a bike or something.

00:11:38.580 --> 00:11:43.320
And it's like, what do you do
make first? Do you make a wheel?

00:11:43.320 --> 00:11:45.840
And you're like, I made
a wheel. Here, it's ready.

00:11:45.840 --> 00:11:47.715
And then you make the seat.

00:11:48.240 --> 00:11:49.320
Here's a seat. Or do you like,

00:11:49.320 --> 00:11:52.920
is the first thing that you
make a really simple skateboard

00:11:52.920 --> 00:11:55.980
thing that someone can use
to get somewhere and then you

00:11:55.980 --> 00:11:56.890
change and change?

00:11:56.890 --> 00:11:57.465
So I think

00:11:58.560 --> 00:12:00.060
it is so important

00:12:02.210 --> 00:12:06.540
to build in such a way
that at any given point,

00:12:06.540 --> 00:12:09.801
there's something that
you can show and

00:12:13.100 --> 00:12:15.560
say, here is the idea of
what it's going to be.

00:12:15.560 --> 00:12:17.880
And I'm going to improve it
in this way and this way,

00:12:17.880 --> 00:12:19.220
and then improve it in ways.

00:12:19.220 --> 00:12:22.230
And it is true that
sometimes this means--

00:12:23.360 --> 00:12:25.970
it can take longer to get
to the eventual thing,

00:12:25.970 --> 00:12:28.235
like the perfect bike
thing that you have.

00:12:30.740 --> 00:12:34.760
It is I think so important
for buy in from stakeholders,

00:12:34.760 --> 00:12:37.400
and it's so important for
people seeing you as someone

00:12:37.400 --> 00:12:38.550
who ships and

00:12:40.700 --> 00:12:41.690
who gets things done.

00:12:41.690 --> 00:12:44.600
And it provides something
useful that you get something

00:12:44.600 --> 00:12:50.950
useful as early as possible,
even if it is not the

00:12:51.830 --> 00:12:53.840
eventual way you're
going to do it.

00:12:53.840 --> 00:12:56.630
I think adopting
that kind of mindset,

00:12:56.630 --> 00:13:00.350
it makes a huge difference
in how collaborative people think

00:13:00.350 --> 00:13:02.570
you are and how responsive
people think you are,

00:13:02.570 --> 00:13:05.045
like your
organization, how your

00:13:05.750 --> 00:13:10.071
position with an organization, if
you can break things down.

00:13:11.780 --> 00:13:13.810
I mean, there's tons of books
and blog posts about this.

00:13:13.810 --> 00:13:17.550
I'm not saying anything crazy or
new but even for something statistical.

00:13:19.278 --> 00:13:20.370
Can I do--

00:13:20.370 --> 00:13:21.580
can I give someone

00:13:22.380 --> 00:13:26.770
something that is dead simple
now? And then can I iterate,

00:13:26.770 --> 00:13:27.810
iterate, iterate.

00:13:27.810 --> 00:13:30.180
Like if you were talking
about a machine learning context,

00:13:30.180 --> 00:13:34.200
it's like can I do something
that I almost even didn't have

00:13:34.200 --> 00:13:36.600
to train or that doesn't
use machine learning,

00:13:36.600 --> 00:13:39.180
like as an initial like here.

00:13:39.180 --> 00:13:41.400
It's going to look like this.
It's going to be this API.

00:13:41.400 --> 00:13:43.330
And right now,
what's serving it is

00:13:44.010 --> 00:13:46.110
something like so
dumb, like so simple.

00:13:46.110 --> 00:13:50.505
And then you do a model
that you can train in a

00:13:51.450 --> 00:13:55.020
literally 10 minutes and then you
do that and then you try to

00:13:55.020 --> 00:13:57.105
improve it rather than to
get to something great.

00:13:58.030 --> 00:14:00.780
I think that that does
have to be balanced with

00:14:02.280 --> 00:14:03.400
how much--

00:14:05.400 --> 00:14:07.800
just time it takes
to do projects.

00:14:07.800 --> 00:14:12.450
But I think within the scope
of whatever kind of project you're doing,

00:14:12.450 --> 00:14:16.130
if you can really think about not
trying to build it up in pieces

00:14:16.950 --> 00:14:19.960
so that the individual
pieces, like that

00:14:20.760 --> 00:14:24.540
they're as useful as
early as possible,

00:14:24.540 --> 00:14:26.325
even if that means
you build it simpler.

00:14:28.060 --> 00:14:28.770
So I think that

00:14:29.550 --> 00:14:31.695
is important. And
that really helps

00:14:32.730 --> 00:14:35.040
the teams I've been on in

00:14:35.880 --> 00:14:37.830
that like maintaining
motivation.

00:14:37.830 --> 00:14:41.430
Because if people are actually
using even the very early

00:14:41.430 --> 00:14:42.840
versions of what you're doing,

00:14:43.560 --> 00:14:46.110
you get feedback on it.
you learn about what--

00:14:46.110 --> 00:14:48.945
you learn earlier what people
are actually looking for.

00:14:50.430 --> 00:14:52.155
And that--

00:14:54.190 --> 00:14:57.560
versus that very siloed
feeling of like, well,

00:14:57.560 --> 00:15:01.720
I'm just going to grind away for
a very long time on this big project.

00:15:01.720 --> 00:15:04.550
But instead of that feeling,
like what about this?

00:15:04.550 --> 00:15:05.740
What about this? Like
getting feedback.

00:15:06.700 --> 00:15:10.540
I think, for teams I
have worked on has been very

00:15:10.540 --> 00:15:13.060
motivating, very motivating.

00:15:13.060 --> 00:15:16.240
- And can I ask that next one, Rachel?

00:15:16.240 --> 00:15:17.860
- Go for it. Yeah. Go for it.

00:15:18.700 --> 00:15:19.120
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:15:19.120 --> 00:15:19.300
- I love--

00:15:19.300 --> 00:15:21.535
I love that explanation
too, though Julia.

00:15:22.750 --> 00:15:22.945
-

00:15:22.945 --> 00:15:25.780
So the ideas that come
about obviously you're not limiting

00:15:25.780 --> 00:15:28.420
yourself to just like to ideas
that you've had over the last

00:15:28.420 --> 00:15:29.540
six or seven years.

00:15:29.540 --> 00:15:33.310
So how do you vet the
ideas that you're coming up with and

00:15:33.310 --> 00:15:35.890
that you're seeing with
yourself and also with your

00:15:35.890 --> 00:15:39.280
team to make sure that they're
valid and they're worth

00:15:39.280 --> 00:15:40.840
creating a vision around?

00:15:40.840 --> 00:15:42.940
- That's a great question.
So you're asking if--

00:15:45.850 --> 00:15:46.930
OK. Yeah. So

00:15:48.460 --> 00:15:49.550
I am also

00:15:50.350 --> 00:15:52.080
a pretty big fan of

00:15:52.960 --> 00:15:58.510
like small experiments, small
experiments and hypotheses. And this

00:15:59.410 --> 00:16:00.230
is related

00:16:00.970 --> 00:16:02.480
to the thing that we
were just talking about

00:16:05.380 --> 00:16:06.670
deciding--

00:16:06.670 --> 00:16:08.500
like getting to something--

00:16:11.020 --> 00:16:11.870
like demo

00:16:12.610 --> 00:16:14.820
level, getting to something--

00:16:15.790 --> 00:16:18.250
there's these minimum
viable project.

00:16:18.250 --> 00:16:19.330
These are the kind of words--

00:16:19.330 --> 00:16:22.660
getting to something
as early as possible.

00:16:22.660 --> 00:16:25.780
Like literally sometimes
timeboxing something to one

00:16:25.780 --> 00:16:27.970
afternoon, one hour, even.

00:16:27.970 --> 00:16:31.270
Like what can I get in one
hour? And can I show this to

00:16:31.270 --> 00:16:35.140
someone and get feedback on it
and see what resonates about it

00:16:35.140 --> 00:16:35.755
and what doesn't?

00:16:38.050 --> 00:16:40.495
So that I think
is like one of the

00:16:41.890 --> 00:16:42.390
biggest

00:16:45.550 --> 00:16:49.810
tools I've used to
evaluate ideas or evaluate hypotheses.

00:16:49.810 --> 00:16:51.280
Like if we try

00:16:52.540 --> 00:16:54.595
something, is it like--

00:16:55.270 --> 00:16:58.570
does it resonate? Does
someone want to see more?

00:16:58.570 --> 00:17:00.130
Does someone want to--

00:17:00.130 --> 00:17:03.430
have changes they want to
suggest it? Or are people like--

00:17:03.430 --> 00:17:04.000
I don't know.

00:17:06.550 --> 00:17:08.000
I am really biased towards

00:17:09.480 --> 00:17:09.980
ship

00:17:10.770 --> 00:17:12.869
early and experiment
and don't have--

00:17:12.869 --> 00:17:14.280
and I just mean that you'd--

00:17:14.280 --> 00:17:15.030
like for me,

00:17:15.030 --> 00:17:17.369
it's like I have to have
an attitude of like holding these

00:17:17.369 --> 00:17:19.560
things with a very light
hand that I'm like--

00:17:19.560 --> 00:17:22.349
I'm not super invested
in this or this, like

00:17:23.069 --> 00:17:26.099
I just want to know. What
do you think? What do you think?

00:17:26.099 --> 00:17:27.105
Like that type of thing.

00:17:28.410 --> 00:17:33.300
So on the positive like example
of this, that is how Vetiver

00:17:33.300 --> 00:17:34.260
happened actually.

00:17:34.260 --> 00:17:34.760
So

00:17:35.700 --> 00:17:36.420
I think--

00:17:36.420 --> 00:17:40.020
I was like, it's like MLOps
as a tool, feels so or--

00:17:40.020 --> 00:17:46.185
as a field or idea feels like
so big, a little hype-driven and what,

00:17:47.580 --> 00:17:48.600
oh, we can't--

00:17:48.600 --> 00:17:51.550
we say, we as Posits,
it's like, can we

00:17:52.350 --> 00:17:55.200
play in that space? Is
that reasonable for us?

00:17:55.200 --> 00:17:57.690
There are whole startups that
that's nothing what they do.

00:17:57.690 --> 00:17:59.580
And like I had a
hypothesis, I was like,

00:18:00.390 --> 00:18:03.840
I think the pieces are
here in open source.

00:18:03.840 --> 00:18:07.320
And people are already kind
of bailing wire these things

00:18:07.320 --> 00:18:08.010
together themselves,

00:18:08.010 --> 00:18:12.880
m if they're not like buying
into a startup product MLOps thing.

00:18:12.880 --> 00:18:16.500
What if we made like an
open source thing that really used

00:18:17.920 --> 00:18:20.960
components, pieces
that are already there?

00:18:20.960 --> 00:18:24.550
And so the initial version that
I like showed someone was like,

00:18:24.550 --> 00:18:26.320
I think we have most
of what we need,

00:18:26.320 --> 00:18:29.380
and we just need to make
like the user experience nice for

00:18:29.380 --> 00:18:32.410
this particular model base
use case. And so that was--

00:18:34.750 --> 00:18:35.440
like the initial--

00:18:35.440 --> 00:18:37.540
I did actually--

00:18:37.540 --> 00:18:39.682
the initial thing I made was

00:18:41.530 --> 00:18:45.040
actually much more Shiny-like
and ended up being,

00:18:46.240 --> 00:18:49.590
in terms of what it
looked, like a server, like

00:18:50.290 --> 00:18:51.020
all this kind of thing.

00:18:51.020 --> 00:18:51.910
And I ended up--

00:18:51.910 --> 00:18:55.320
I ended up there ended up being
a fairly big shift of like, oh,

00:18:55.320 --> 00:18:58.435
OK. No, no, no. We're not going
to do it that way. We're going to

00:19:00.130 --> 00:19:01.195
instead do like

00:19:01.840 --> 00:19:03.010
code generation,

00:19:03.010 --> 00:19:05.230
a fair amount of code
generation behind the scene--

00:19:05.860 --> 00:19:07.600
so I shifted.

00:19:07.600 --> 00:19:11.860
But there were things about
the initial version I showed people

00:19:11.860 --> 00:19:15.100
that resonated. They were
like, OK, they want to do X.

00:19:15.100 --> 00:19:16.150
And then there were
things about it.

00:19:16.150 --> 00:19:17.890
They were like, I don't know.

00:19:17.890 --> 00:19:20.560
Like that's not making sense
to me or like that seems too

00:19:20.560 --> 00:19:22.570
complicated. Like I think
I should be simpler.

00:19:22.570 --> 00:19:25.630
And so there were things about
it that helped me iterate

00:19:25.630 --> 00:19:26.610
towards something.

00:19:28.180 --> 00:19:29.830
And then we iterate, iterate,

00:19:29.830 --> 00:19:33.730
and keep going forward and land
on something that hopefully fits pretty

00:19:34.690 --> 00:19:36.640
well. So I think that--

00:19:40.690 --> 00:19:41.590
I probably

00:19:42.940 --> 00:19:43.440
lean

00:19:44.790 --> 00:19:45.290
less,

00:19:46.630 --> 00:19:49.310
I would say, on my own

00:19:51.070 --> 00:19:54.400
opinions or tastes unlike,
I am sure this is right.

00:19:55.210 --> 00:19:56.560
And I tend to--

00:19:57.520 --> 00:20:02.260
I tend to lean more on, let
me like show things to people, let

00:20:02.260 --> 00:20:06.190
me do even user interviews,
like let me like

00:20:07.030 --> 00:20:09.100
use some of those tools to

00:20:09.760 --> 00:20:11.515
figure out how things
need to happen.

00:20:12.560 --> 00:20:13.060
- Yeah.

00:20:13.690 --> 00:20:16.840
I see there's a
little conversation in the chat

00:20:16.840 --> 00:20:17.950
happening about this too.

00:20:17.950 --> 00:20:20.740
And we can definitely jump
on to other topics too.

00:20:20.740 --> 00:20:23.440
But I wanted to make
sure while that conversation is happening,

00:20:23.440 --> 00:20:24.780
we dive into.

00:20:25.540 --> 00:20:28.405
George, do you want to add
your follow on question?

00:20:29.670 --> 00:20:30.615
- Yeah. Yeah.

00:20:31.215 --> 00:20:36.030
I guess I've had the
experience where people really don't want

00:20:36.030 --> 00:20:37.950
to see those
intermediate steps that

00:20:39.000 --> 00:20:40.935
just want that final thing,

00:20:41.800 --> 00:20:42.960
especially to your point about

00:20:44.241 --> 00:20:46.780
even in cases where
it's a statistical

00:20:48.230 --> 00:20:52.440
model or something that
you're needing to deliver. Sometimes,

00:20:52.440 --> 00:20:54.525
delivering a simple
thing is good enough.

00:20:55.300 --> 00:20:56.940
I've had the experience
where it's just not.

00:20:57.780 --> 00:21:02.475
And I think it's because
of perception of stability or

00:21:03.780 --> 00:21:06.250
maybe just the culture.

00:21:07.440 --> 00:21:07.940
- Yeah.

00:21:08.650 --> 00:21:11.925
- And so I don't know if
you've ever come across that. But it's like,

00:21:12.570 --> 00:21:16.200
it's basically like you have to
sell the whole thing or else it

00:21:16.200 --> 00:21:18.280
dies. And I don't know if

00:21:19.020 --> 00:21:21.510
you ever had that experience
and how you've overcome that.

00:21:21.510 --> 00:21:25.035
- Yeah. Yeah. Yeah. So
this experience where

00:21:26.040 --> 00:21:26.540
someone

00:21:27.760 --> 00:21:30.000
doesn't view it as important

00:21:30.880 --> 00:21:33.580
to give feedback on an
intermediate thing or to look

00:21:33.580 --> 00:21:35.290
at an intermediate thing or to

00:21:35.950 --> 00:21:37.650
start integrating
something that

00:21:38.650 --> 00:21:42.355
is less complete or less
mature or less finished

00:21:43.900 --> 00:21:47.485
into maybe the system
that you're going for.

00:21:48.790 --> 00:21:50.680
So if it is something--

00:21:50.680 --> 00:21:52.450
if it is really something
that you're like,

00:21:54.106 --> 00:21:55.120
I need--

00:21:55.120 --> 00:21:59.170
I need feedback on these
intermediate steps in order to

00:21:59.170 --> 00:22:00.355
get where I need to go,

00:22:03.350 --> 00:22:05.560
it can be helpful to

00:22:07.160 --> 00:22:07.660
explain

00:22:08.660 --> 00:22:09.160
why.

00:22:09.980 --> 00:22:13.050
Like explain why we want to

00:22:15.620 --> 00:22:16.685
build things

00:22:17.360 --> 00:22:22.235
with an iterative improvement.
If you're talking to a

00:22:23.180 --> 00:22:24.590
software engineer colleague,

00:22:24.590 --> 00:22:26.570
they probably resonate
with this idea.

00:22:26.570 --> 00:22:28.220
Because it's so common
in software engineering,

00:22:28.220 --> 00:22:31.970
this idea of we do
something small, then we improve it.

00:22:31.970 --> 00:22:33.560
Like we don't do something--

00:22:33.560 --> 00:22:37.460
we don't do something huge
and massive all at once.

00:22:37.460 --> 00:22:39.230
Like we know that's
more prone to errors.

00:22:39.230 --> 00:22:42.650
It's more prone to not
getting where you want to be.

00:22:42.650 --> 00:22:43.560
So you can--

00:22:43.560 --> 00:22:47.630
so I have had luck with
software engineer colleagues in

00:22:47.630 --> 00:22:52.280
explaining this is like
what you do but for data

00:22:52.280 --> 00:22:54.065
work. So I need you to--

00:22:55.670 --> 00:22:56.375
I need

00:22:57.350 --> 00:23:00.200
us to take these steps so that
we end up in a better place in

00:23:00.200 --> 00:23:06.465
the long run. I think that
it might be interesting, George, to--

00:23:07.860 --> 00:23:11.940
if you're having this problem
more with say like a business

00:23:11.940 --> 00:23:13.736
stakeholder, like
less with an engineer,

00:23:14.450 --> 00:23:17.160
an engineering stakeholder in your
day to work and more like

00:23:17.160 --> 00:23:18.660
a business stakeholder.

00:23:19.380 --> 00:23:19.880
And

00:23:20.490 --> 00:23:24.360
I have had more challenges
in those situations in trying to--

00:23:26.160 --> 00:23:27.825
and trying to get

00:23:30.000 --> 00:23:31.500
those kinds of answers.

00:23:32.310 --> 00:23:36.600
So sometimes, I have been
in a situation where just share

00:23:38.020 --> 00:23:38.520
and

00:23:40.540 --> 00:23:42.362
partly, and as a like,

00:23:43.126 --> 00:23:47.150
here's where it is. And the
next steps are X next week.

00:23:47.150 --> 00:23:50.140
Here's where it is and next
steps are Y. If I do need

00:23:50.140 --> 00:23:51.550
feedback, if I do need--

00:23:51.550 --> 00:23:52.050
I

00:23:53.470 --> 00:23:56.150
need something back
the other direction,

00:23:59.520 --> 00:24:03.650
it can be more challenging
with business stakeholders

00:24:04.350 --> 00:24:05.280
to understand that.

00:24:05.280 --> 00:24:05.780
But

00:24:06.600 --> 00:24:10.110
when I have had
success with that,

00:24:10.110 --> 00:24:12.120
overcoming that
kind of obstacle,

00:24:12.120 --> 00:24:15.420
it has been through
a fairly specific

00:24:16.910 --> 00:24:19.400
request or like a
fairly specific--

00:24:22.124 --> 00:24:23.885
I have a--

00:24:23.885 --> 00:24:26.435
I have a prototype of

00:24:27.470 --> 00:24:29.690
the statistical
results. It's not--

00:24:29.690 --> 00:24:33.140
it's in its final version but
I need to ask you these questions

00:24:33.140 --> 00:24:36.425
about it. And if either set
a meeting or if it's async like

00:24:37.100 --> 00:24:38.870
a question A, question
B, question C.

00:24:38.870 --> 00:24:42.935
And then when I've
had success, it's been--

00:24:44.370 --> 00:24:47.490
like I really clearly
understand why I need something.

00:24:47.490 --> 00:24:50.270
It's like the goal is
this. To get to the goal,

00:24:50.270 --> 00:24:52.100
I need to know which
of these things too.

00:24:52.100 --> 00:24:53.330
But that can be a more--

00:24:53.330 --> 00:24:54.920
because there's less
of a culture I think.

00:24:56.660 --> 00:24:57.980
With engineers,

00:24:57.980 --> 00:25:00.410
there's more of a
norm and expected culture around.

00:25:00.410 --> 00:25:03.060
We do things iteratively
to get a better result.

00:25:03.060 --> 00:25:05.660
But I have had more.
It has been more challenging there.

00:25:05.660 --> 00:25:07.980
And then I think
connecting it to

00:25:08.660 --> 00:25:11.225
what is the goal and saying
like this is what I need

00:25:11.960 --> 00:25:13.350
has been how I have

00:25:14.030 --> 00:25:15.110
had success in that.

00:25:15.110 --> 00:25:17.970
But I have sometimes been in
a situation where just feel like,

00:25:17.970 --> 00:25:20.100
am I just putting this
out into the void?

00:25:22.110 --> 00:25:22.230
-

00:25:22.230 --> 00:25:25.710
It's an interesting thought to
think about how we get other

00:25:25.710 --> 00:25:28.035
people to think
iteratively as well.

00:25:29.190 --> 00:25:30.240
But Husada,

00:25:30.240 --> 00:25:33.450
I see you had a question a
bit earlier about tidymodels.

00:25:33.450 --> 00:25:34.830
Do you want to jump in?

00:25:34.830 --> 00:25:37.890
And I can read the question and
if you want to jump in and add

00:25:37.890 --> 00:25:40.260
more context, feel free.
But the question was,

00:25:40.260 --> 00:25:44.850
how are the state of Tidymodels
adaptations? Are there

00:25:44.850 --> 00:25:48.720
enough adopters or users
to please the Tidymodels teams

00:25:48.720 --> 00:25:50.880
metric or target?
Are there targets?

00:25:51.720 --> 00:25:53.175
- Yeah. That's a
great question.

00:25:54.015 --> 00:25:54.790
Much so,

00:25:55.530 --> 00:25:56.670
I think that

00:25:58.530 --> 00:25:59.550
it is--

00:26:00.990 --> 00:26:02.400
so when I have been--

00:26:03.330 --> 00:26:05.500
while I have worked on
open source projects,

00:26:06.660 --> 00:26:07.160
adoption

00:26:08.550 --> 00:26:10.265
is something that

00:26:12.540 --> 00:26:14.250
open source teams watch,

00:26:14.250 --> 00:26:17.295
that open source teams
pay attention to, and decide.

00:26:19.160 --> 00:26:19.370
Well,

00:26:19.370 --> 00:26:24.230
it's like one piece of data
we have to decide how to invest

00:26:24.230 --> 00:26:26.075
and what to invest in. Like if

00:26:28.400 --> 00:26:28.700
you're there,

00:26:28.700 --> 00:26:31.460
are like two things we can
invest in our projects,

00:26:31.460 --> 00:26:33.090
we invest in. How

00:26:33.830 --> 00:26:37.205
do we prioritize them? One of
the things that goes into is

00:26:38.600 --> 00:26:39.870
things like adoption.

00:26:39.870 --> 00:26:40.820
It's not the only thing,

00:26:40.820 --> 00:26:42.875
because sometimes that's
not the only reason to

00:26:43.520 --> 00:26:44.960
invest in something.

00:26:45.830 --> 00:26:50.210
Like I talk about this all
the time, all the time.

00:26:50.210 --> 00:26:53.150
But you probably are familiar
with the idea of the data

00:26:53.150 --> 00:26:55.010
science hierarchy of needs.

00:26:55.010 --> 00:26:59.490
Monica Rogati wrote about this
in 2017 or 16 or something.

00:26:59.490 --> 00:27:02.540
And at the bottom is like,
you're collecting data at all.

00:27:02.540 --> 00:27:04.730
And then one up
is you're doing--

00:27:04.730 --> 00:27:07.490
you're doing analytics,
things like dashboards.

00:27:07.490 --> 00:27:10.085
One like a level up
from that is like--

00:27:13.470 --> 00:27:16.370
is like statistical analysis,

00:27:16.370 --> 00:27:20.430
like maybe things like a T-test
or maybe something like a linear model.

00:27:20.430 --> 00:27:22.380
And above that, you're
using machine learning.

00:27:22.380 --> 00:27:24.675
And at the top is like AI

00:27:25.320 --> 00:27:28.950
kind of thing. And you can think
about that also in terms of, where

00:27:29.850 --> 00:27:33.180
does model deployment go on
that? Like it's up pretty high.

00:27:33.180 --> 00:27:34.665
Like people have to be using--

00:27:36.000 --> 00:27:37.410
have to be having models.

00:27:37.410 --> 00:27:39.150
They need to have models
that are deployed.

00:27:39.150 --> 00:27:42.610
Like it's up pretty high
in that hierarchy of needs.

00:27:43.320 --> 00:27:45.390
So that affects
adoption numbers.

00:27:45.390 --> 00:27:48.870
Like something like Tidymodels
is never going to have the

00:27:48.870 --> 00:27:50.760
adoption numbers of
something like Shiny.

00:27:50.760 --> 00:27:54.390
Because they're at different
places in the data science

00:27:54.390 --> 00:27:55.620
hierarchy of needs.

00:27:57.840 --> 00:27:59.010
But at the same time,

00:27:59.010 --> 00:28:00.840
like if you're thinking about
what should we invest in?

00:28:00.840 --> 00:28:04.720
You want to make sure that
when people look at tooling or

00:28:05.400 --> 00:28:06.960
a language or

00:28:07.830 --> 00:28:10.830
a company's products or
something, that when people

00:28:10.830 --> 00:28:13.800
look at it, they can see
that it can grow with them.

00:28:13.800 --> 00:28:17.550
They can see that this is not
a tool that like maxes out.

00:28:18.270 --> 00:28:19.780
Or if you do decide to do that,

00:28:19.780 --> 00:28:21.000
you need to do it
very carefully.

00:28:21.000 --> 00:28:22.590
Like when people
look at this tool,

00:28:23.370 --> 00:28:25.350
they're going to understand
its scope is limited.

00:28:25.350 --> 00:28:27.600
And they can't use
it beyond X level

00:28:28.320 --> 00:28:30.750
of scale or maturity
or whatever.

00:28:30.750 --> 00:28:32.250
So there's different reasons.

00:28:32.250 --> 00:28:34.950
I would say that in
terms of adoption--

00:28:36.930 --> 00:28:37.430
that

00:28:40.590 --> 00:28:42.060
when we look at who

00:28:43.500 --> 00:28:47.260
wants to come to conf and learn
about something or like who

00:28:48.300 --> 00:28:49.530
if we look at-- like

00:28:49.530 --> 00:28:50.030
who,

00:28:50.760 --> 00:28:53.620
like internally, and our customers,
like how often are people

00:28:54.360 --> 00:28:58.680
asking about a tool or wanting
like to know more about how to

00:28:58.680 --> 00:29:00.230
do something. We

00:29:01.680 --> 00:29:07.530
feel good about the investment
that has been made in Tidymodels.

00:29:07.530 --> 00:29:10.680
Certainly, certainly,
when you think about--

00:29:11.730 --> 00:29:15.600
if you were to go right now
to GitHub and like count up like

00:29:15.600 --> 00:29:17.930
how many people or how
many things are using

00:29:19.340 --> 00:29:22.430
Tidymodels versus something
else, which you might--

00:29:22.430 --> 00:29:25.070
what would you compare it to?
Would you compare it to Carrot?

00:29:25.070 --> 00:29:28.520
Would you compare it to
Scikit Learn? Like probably,

00:29:28.520 --> 00:29:30.620
if you were to compare
those numbers, you'd be like, oh,

00:29:30.620 --> 00:29:33.380
there's more projects that
use Carrot because Carrot's have

00:29:33.380 --> 00:29:37.490
been around for like 15 more
years or something like that or

00:29:37.490 --> 00:29:40.490
Scikit Learn, many
more projects,

00:29:40.490 --> 00:29:42.380
for reasons that we understand.

00:29:42.380 --> 00:29:42.880
But

00:29:43.550 --> 00:29:46.725
so I think that if
you ask the question, like is

00:29:48.150 --> 00:29:51.680
Posit happy about the
investment that has been made

00:29:51.680 --> 00:29:55.040
in Tidymodels? I think
it really fits a need,

00:29:55.040 --> 00:29:56.880
like a need that people have.

00:29:56.880 --> 00:30:01.190
And so in that sense has
been really happy about the investment.

00:30:01.190 --> 00:30:06.080
- Awesome. Julia, a question I wanted
to ask you is, I see a

00:30:06.080 --> 00:30:10.190
lot of things on LinkedIn that
are like models are rarely

00:30:10.190 --> 00:30:13.970
deployed or oh, teams spent
all this time developing something,

00:30:13.970 --> 00:30:16.755
and it doesn't go anywhere.
Why does that happen?

00:30:18.070 --> 00:30:21.270
- I think some of the
time that's appropriate.

00:30:21.270 --> 00:30:22.960
Like that's normal and OK.

00:30:23.700 --> 00:30:24.200
Because

00:30:25.620 --> 00:30:28.070
one thing that's
different about

00:30:29.550 --> 00:30:32.100
machine learning as a
practice compared to software

00:30:32.100 --> 00:30:35.550
engineering as a practice is
you can pretty much always

00:30:37.210 --> 00:30:37.910
get--

00:30:37.910 --> 00:30:39.760
you can pretty much
always build something.

00:30:39.760 --> 00:30:43.630
Like if you write out
a spec for something that's like,

00:30:43.630 --> 00:30:46.570
we need to build X like
as a software engineer.

00:30:46.570 --> 00:30:48.325
Let's write out a spec. Let's

00:30:49.390 --> 00:30:50.320
do it.

00:30:50.320 --> 00:30:54.130
Like you pretty much
always can do that.

00:30:55.150 --> 00:30:57.970
Maybe it will take longer than you
think or more investment than you think,

00:30:57.970 --> 00:31:00.610
but actually that's not true
when it comes to machine

00:31:00.610 --> 00:31:03.910
learning. Because until
you start the process,

00:31:03.910 --> 00:31:08.020
you don't necessarily know that
you can predict the thing that

00:31:08.020 --> 00:31:10.690
you need to predict from
the stuff that you have--

00:31:10.690 --> 00:31:16.420
you end up, may end up not
having appropriate data. You may end up--

00:31:17.930 --> 00:31:19.480
it is not good enough.

00:31:19.480 --> 00:31:21.820
Like the results you get are
not good enough to predict that

00:31:21.820 --> 00:31:23.095
you think that you want to.

00:31:25.090 --> 00:31:26.540
So machine learning

00:31:27.790 --> 00:31:31.270
as a practice is
it's more iterative.

00:31:31.790 --> 00:31:33.100
It's more exploratory.

00:31:33.910 --> 00:31:36.860
We're not even talking
specifically about exploratory

00:31:36.860 --> 00:31:40.340
data analysis. But as
a practice, it is more

00:31:40.340 --> 00:31:43.460
exploratory than say, we're
going to build a feature.

00:31:43.460 --> 00:31:45.725
We're going to build
a feature. And it is--

00:31:47.300 --> 00:31:50.720
you know you can be
less certain ahead of time that

00:31:50.720 --> 00:31:51.815
whatever model

00:31:52.670 --> 00:31:54.510
you're trying to do
you can actually fit,

00:31:54.510 --> 00:31:57.060
especially within
whatever is needed.

00:31:57.060 --> 00:31:59.540
So I think that it
happens because--

00:31:59.540 --> 00:32:03.800
that that's a big reason why
it happens that is the nature

00:32:04.640 --> 00:32:07.680
of data science and nature
of machine learning.

00:32:07.680 --> 00:32:09.770
There's a really great paper.

00:32:10.940 --> 00:32:12.740
I'm going to see if
I can find it real

00:32:13.400 --> 00:32:15.305
quick, that talks about this.

00:32:19.520 --> 00:32:24.530
Yes. OK. I found it. It's
called operationalizing machine

00:32:24.530 --> 00:32:29.150
learning. And I'll put the
link here, and it talks about--

00:32:29.150 --> 00:32:31.770
it talks about this in
the context of machine learning,

00:32:31.770 --> 00:32:35.720
especially and really
just says, this is why.

00:32:35.720 --> 00:32:40.090
Like this is why is because
of the nature of this work.

00:32:40.820 --> 00:32:42.310
So that's one big reason.

00:32:42.310 --> 00:32:46.510
I think another reason is
that it often when people are

00:32:46.510 --> 00:32:49.810
working on machine learning
projects in their org,

00:32:49.810 --> 00:32:53.170
they're bumping up against the
maturity of where they've been

00:32:53.170 --> 00:32:56.700
before, where their org has
been before. And so peoples

00:32:58.690 --> 00:33:03.610
don't have as much of the
muscle memory and the skills

00:33:03.610 --> 00:33:07.190
about how to do these
things, how to deploy them.

00:33:07.190 --> 00:33:08.200
So that's I think--

00:33:08.200 --> 00:33:08.860
so the biggest--

00:33:08.860 --> 00:33:10.300
I think big reason is like,

00:33:10.300 --> 00:33:12.730
it is the nature of
machine learning as work.

00:33:12.730 --> 00:33:15.190
The next piece is that
often when people are doing these

00:33:15.190 --> 00:33:18.040
things, it's new to
their org or newer--

00:33:19.420 --> 00:33:19.920
they're

00:33:20.500 --> 00:33:21.880
exercising new skills.

00:33:21.880 --> 00:33:25.375
And the other thing I think
that really contributes to this is

00:33:26.590 --> 00:33:30.460
you almost always are ending
up collaborating across

00:33:30.460 --> 00:33:33.730
functions. So most typically,
like you are needing--

00:33:34.450 --> 00:33:36.040
if you're a more
statistical person,

00:33:36.040 --> 00:33:38.270
you are now needing to
collaborate with someone who is

00:33:38.270 --> 00:33:43.280
a more ops-y person. And
people work in different ways. Teams

00:33:43.280 --> 00:33:44.960
don't sit together. Like

00:33:45.860 --> 00:33:47.360
people end up with--

00:33:47.360 --> 00:33:49.310
oh, that will never work.

00:33:49.310 --> 00:33:50.660
Like that will never
work because of X.

00:33:52.235 --> 00:33:55.640
And what can both sides do
to actually make something work if

00:33:55.640 --> 00:33:56.220
it needs to happen.

00:33:56.220 --> 00:34:00.350
So I think those are the real
reasons why this kind of thing happens.

00:34:00.350 --> 00:34:01.445
- Yeah. Thank you.

00:34:02.220 --> 00:34:02.990
I see, Patrick,

00:34:02.990 --> 00:34:06.200
you had asked a question that
I think follows along to this

00:34:06.200 --> 00:34:08.015
nicely. If you want
to jump in here too.

00:34:09.300 --> 00:34:12.840
- Thanks, Rachel. And Julia, hi.
It's good to see you. Yeah.

00:34:12.840 --> 00:34:14.850
This is very much what you
were just talking about and

00:34:14.850 --> 00:34:19.050
references back to the data
science hierarchy of needs piece too.

00:34:19.050 --> 00:34:21.719
So I'm just wondering what
advice would you give to a team

00:34:21.719 --> 00:34:24.690
or an organization who's
struggling to go from, OK,

00:34:24.690 --> 00:34:26.370
we have a model and
it fits well enough.

00:34:26.370 --> 00:34:29.909
And it's doing what we want
it to do to having a model in

00:34:29.909 --> 00:34:32.670
production that we're using
regularly and that we depend

00:34:32.670 --> 00:34:35.415
on. That it feels like
a very big change to me

00:34:36.239 --> 00:34:39.300
psychologically for the team or
the organization to go from.

00:34:39.300 --> 00:34:41.639
Yes, we can do this to--

00:34:41.639 --> 00:34:43.989
yes, we are relying
on this thing.

00:34:43.989 --> 00:34:46.050
And it is in production
and it's important to us.

00:34:46.050 --> 00:34:48.900
So what advice do you have for
people who are struggling to make that step?

00:34:48.900 --> 00:34:52.290
- Yeah. Yeah. So I think
one thing is to acknowledge,

00:34:52.290 --> 00:34:55.889
to understand that you as a
team or some part of the team

00:34:55.889 --> 00:34:57.389
is going to need to

00:34:58.110 --> 00:34:59.670
gain some new skills,

00:34:59.670 --> 00:35:03.210
is going to need to learn how
to do some new things and to

00:35:03.210 --> 00:35:06.314
try to make space
and understand that that

00:35:07.200 --> 00:35:12.520
kind of change is coming and
is needed. So this is, for example,

00:35:14.020 --> 00:35:18.150
like maybe it's important for someone
on your team to start in

00:35:18.880 --> 00:35:19.380
learning

00:35:20.710 --> 00:35:22.870
what does our org need?

00:35:22.870 --> 00:35:24.340
If I were to deploy a model,

00:35:24.340 --> 00:35:26.350
like what is going
to work for our org?

00:35:26.350 --> 00:35:29.830
So this is now working
with maybe people who are DevOps-y,

00:35:29.830 --> 00:35:32.410
maybe people who are sit-in IT.

00:35:34.040 --> 00:35:37.570
So what are the new
skills we need to add?

00:35:37.570 --> 00:35:40.960
So that's one thing is to
acknowledge that if you have

00:35:40.960 --> 00:35:43.960
been very statistically focused
and it's time to deploy a

00:35:43.960 --> 00:35:48.070
model, how can we make space
for some proportion of our team

00:35:48.070 --> 00:35:51.280
to gain some of those skills?

00:35:51.280 --> 00:35:54.490
I think also to not be afraid
of some of those tools and

00:35:54.490 --> 00:35:55.585
skills and to

00:35:56.320 --> 00:35:58.870
understand that
like that's how you

00:36:00.640 --> 00:36:04.892
scale the impact of your work.
So for example, like to learn

00:36:06.910 --> 00:36:08.980
about Docker, like
to learn about--

00:36:12.480 --> 00:36:15.250
how do things work in your
org? If your org has a

00:36:16.170 --> 00:36:17.870
certain Cloud platform, that

00:36:18.690 --> 00:36:20.550
you all use, like to start--

00:36:20.550 --> 00:36:23.335
to not so much be like,
OK, yes, we're the data people.

00:36:24.330 --> 00:36:29.280
We work in this environment. But to
start to push again, like to find--

00:36:29.280 --> 00:36:32.790
how do I go? How do I
then take that next step?

00:36:32.790 --> 00:36:37.240
Also, I think doing things in
as small of way as possible.

00:36:37.240 --> 00:36:38.460
So like you can--

00:36:39.690 --> 00:36:42.310
if you're going to deploy
some kind of a model as

00:36:44.460 --> 00:36:45.053
an API.

00:36:45.760 --> 00:36:50.220
So like, it doesn't have to
the first time you do it be the

00:36:50.220 --> 00:36:51.517
huge scalable,

00:36:52.710 --> 00:36:56.550
can go to infinity kind of
API. Like you can first do

00:36:57.825 --> 00:36:59.685
API at all. And then you show

00:37:00.510 --> 00:37:02.940
like a software engineer
or DevOps-y kind of coworker,

00:37:02.940 --> 00:37:07.320
like look, look. If we serve
this in this way, then you can

00:37:07.320 --> 00:37:08.890
get predictions
back in this way.

00:37:08.890 --> 00:37:12.410
And then have a discussion about how
can we start to scale that up.

00:37:12.410 --> 00:37:15.630
So I think the first thing
is to think about the skills

00:37:16.390 --> 00:37:19.030
that people need someone
needs to gain a little bit.

00:37:19.030 --> 00:37:20.515
The second thing
is to think about

00:37:21.790 --> 00:37:23.720
infrastructure and like

00:37:24.400 --> 00:37:26.050
the tools you use now,

00:37:26.050 --> 00:37:27.880
how are they different from
the tools that are being there.

00:37:27.880 --> 00:37:30.640
Like what can you learn about
your internal specifics?

00:37:30.640 --> 00:37:32.920
And then also going back
to that like iterative,

00:37:32.920 --> 00:37:37.280
like do something as small as
possible to show and be like,

00:37:37.280 --> 00:37:38.500
OK, how can we make--

00:37:38.500 --> 00:37:39.580
take that next step.

00:37:40.270 --> 00:37:42.170
- Got it. Thank you very much,
Julia. I appreciate that.

00:37:42.170 --> 00:37:44.830
And thank you for building all
the tools that make that stuff

00:37:44.830 --> 00:37:45.985
possible and easier.

