00:43:08.470 --> 00:43:09.970
- And Lisa's back. [INTERPOSING

00:43:09.970 --> 00:43:10.300
VOICES] Feel

00:43:10.300 --> 00:43:12.400
free to jump in
and say hi, Lisa.

00:43:12.400 --> 00:43:16.600
- So I would say for the
teams, I have been on we absolutely

00:43:16.600 --> 00:43:18.550
have considered it part--

00:43:18.550 --> 00:43:19.360
one--

00:43:19.360 --> 00:43:19.930
I'm just going to-- one,

00:43:19.930 --> 00:43:20.905
one, one second.

00:43:22.060 --> 00:43:23.350
Awesome. Thank you so much.

00:43:24.490 --> 00:43:26.230
So absolutely,

00:43:26.230 --> 00:43:30.010
absolutely the teams I have been
on consider it part of our job.

00:43:30.010 --> 00:43:33.490
I do think it depends on
the maturity of the project that

00:43:33.490 --> 00:43:35.305
you're working on.
So for example,

00:43:38.290 --> 00:43:42.140
Hadley has told me, like when
he was first working on ggplot2,

00:43:42.860 --> 00:43:45.800
he answered every Stack
Overflow question about ggplot2

00:43:45.800 --> 00:43:47.840
or at least he looked
at it. He looked at it.

00:43:47.840 --> 00:43:49.250
He made sure that--

00:43:49.250 --> 00:43:52.520
and because it's really
valuable source of feedback.

00:43:52.520 --> 00:43:54.170
Like it's a really
valuable source of--

00:43:54.910 --> 00:43:59.600
it is actually good for you.
But now, ggplot2 is very mature.

00:43:59.600 --> 00:44:02.400
And there's many people
who answer questions on Stack

00:44:02.400 --> 00:44:06.330
Overflow about ggplot2 and so
Hadley doesn't do it anymore.

00:44:06.330 --> 00:44:11.190
So I have set up
like RSS feeds for

00:44:11.870 --> 00:44:14.470
Posit community questions,
for Stack Overflow

00:44:15.410 --> 00:44:18.230
that come to a
place that I check,

00:44:18.230 --> 00:44:20.840
like I have it going
to an RSS feed reader.

00:44:20.840 --> 00:44:21.695
And so I--

00:44:24.560 --> 00:44:25.460
like I literally--

00:44:25.460 --> 00:44:28.040
every time there's a question
on Stack Overflow that's tagged

00:44:28.040 --> 00:44:30.050
Tidymodels, I look at it.

00:44:30.620 --> 00:44:33.530
I look at it, every single
one of them. And sometimes,

00:44:33.530 --> 00:44:35.840
someone has beat me to
answering it. And that's fine.

00:44:35.840 --> 00:44:37.430
Unless they answer it wrong.

00:44:37.430 --> 00:44:38.730
And so that's totally fine.

00:44:38.730 --> 00:44:40.490
But I just want to
make sure anyone who

00:44:41.730 --> 00:44:44.460
comes and is having a
problem, we know what it is.

00:44:44.460 --> 00:44:45.460
We know what it is.

00:44:45.460 --> 00:44:48.180
It's really interesting working
on open source software from

00:44:48.180 --> 00:44:52.500
the perspective of how do you decide
what to do and what the problems are.

00:44:52.500 --> 00:44:53.000
Because

00:44:53.790 --> 00:44:58.605
lots of you I think probably work
in a situation where you have data.

00:44:59.610 --> 00:45:02.220
That's why you're hired.
There's data that exists.

00:45:02.220 --> 00:45:05.730
And you use that data
to improve some product or to

00:45:05.730 --> 00:45:07.800
change something. When
you're working on open source

00:45:07.800 --> 00:45:10.080
software, just by
the nature of it,

00:45:10.080 --> 00:45:12.370
we don't have data about usage.

00:45:12.370 --> 00:45:15.360
We don't have data about
what most common errors are.

00:45:15.360 --> 00:45:18.310
So you have to find
proxies for that.

00:45:18.310 --> 00:45:20.670
And I think things like
questions on Posits community,

00:45:20.670 --> 00:45:24.480
questions on Stack Overflow,
certainly GitHub issues.

00:45:24.480 --> 00:45:28.650
Like these are the proxies that
we have for what problems are

00:45:28.650 --> 00:45:32.550
people having. And so I
think it is absolutely--

00:45:32.550 --> 00:45:34.762
yes, part of the
job of figuring

00:45:36.090 --> 00:45:39.130
out what people's problems are.

00:45:39.130 --> 00:45:42.700
And like I said, this,
especially applies to early

00:45:42.700 --> 00:45:44.815
maturity projects that--

00:45:48.100 --> 00:45:51.212
in fact incredibly valuable
data that you can use to

00:45:52.630 --> 00:45:55.660
decide what it is that you
need to do. It definitely-- you

00:45:55.660 --> 00:45:56.290
have to have--

00:45:56.290 --> 00:46:00.360
you have to have a certain
mindset if you're going into this. Because

00:46:01.060 --> 00:46:02.710
people ask--

00:46:02.710 --> 00:46:05.710
it turns out people ask
the same question over and over.

00:46:05.710 --> 00:46:09.070
No. But like the fact is
like either you need to--

00:46:09.070 --> 00:46:09.790
if someone's--

00:46:09.790 --> 00:46:11.890
if you are finding multiple
people are asking the same

00:46:11.890 --> 00:46:12.850
question over and over,

00:46:13.540 --> 00:46:14.800
you should fix that.

00:46:15.370 --> 00:46:19.360
You should fix that or better
document that or make it easier

00:46:19.360 --> 00:46:22.420
for people to not have to
ask that question anymore.

00:46:22.420 --> 00:46:25.210
But definitely,
definitely, if you're in--

00:46:25.210 --> 00:46:27.700
that's that part of the job
of. OK, let me go through--

00:46:29.170 --> 00:46:31.510
here, here, here. Like all
the questions and feedback,

00:46:31.510 --> 00:46:32.950
let me know--

00:46:32.950 --> 00:46:35.740
let me keep in mind that
person is frustrated,

00:46:35.740 --> 00:46:37.120
a person is having a problem.

00:46:38.140 --> 00:46:42.340
Even if I think, oh, my
gosh, I've answered this five times.

00:46:42.340 --> 00:46:45.130
They have not had it
answered five times.

00:46:45.130 --> 00:46:49.870
So it's definitely a the
dynamic there is one to be

00:46:49.870 --> 00:46:51.150
aware of, I think.

00:46:51.150 --> 00:46:51.170
-

00:46:51.170 --> 00:46:54.220
I think it's a good
reminder for whatever role you're in

00:46:54.220 --> 00:46:57.160
when people keep asking you
the same question. It might be

00:46:57.790 --> 00:46:59.080
or maybe there's a reason.

00:46:59.080 --> 00:47:01.765
- Yeah. Right. Exactly. Exactly.

00:47:03.400 --> 00:47:05.690
- OK. I know this time
goes by super quickly.

